          "lesson": {
            "prerequisite_note": "No IOS command knowledge is required. Read severity as urgency and use the native policy controls. This is an offline simulation, not a real logging service.",
            "concepts": [
              {
                "title": "An event is something that happened",
                "text": "A switch can produce an event when a port changes state. A local buffer stores accepted events on that source; a remote collector stores accepted events sent to it. Generate simulated event creates only the selected supplied example event."
              },
              {
                "title": "Smaller severity numbers are more urgent",
                "text": "Severity0 is most urgent and7 is debug detail. A threshold of4 accepts0 through4, including error3. It filters out5 through7. Local and remote thresholds are separate; the collector can filter again."
              },
              {
                "title": "Two different meanings of facility",
                "text": "LINK in %LINK-4-CHANGED identifies the message subsystem. The exported RFC facility is a separate number, such as20 (local4). A collector selector uses that exported facility, not the LINK label."
              },
              {
                "title": "Edit, apply, then check",
                "text": "Choose a component and edit its native settings. Apply settings commits the complete selected editor. Check configuration tests current acceptance and rejection, including events you have not generated. Historical log entries do not prove that the current settings still work."
              }
            ],
            "description": "On the separate Practice switch, send error3 events to the Practice collector with facility20 (local4), while keeping debug7 out of both logs. The event and addresses differ from the assignment.",
